# Portability patches (format and list: docs/UPSTREAM_CHANGES.md).
patch: ryde-mat-fq-word-size
why: rbc_*_mat_fq_init allocates the matrix words, which are uint64_t, with sizeof(uint64_t *): the same
why: 8 bytes on 64-bit targets, but 4 bytes on 32-bit wasm32, so the matrix gets half the memory it uses
why: and the heap is overwritten (wasm32 abort in free from rbc_*_mat_fq_clear, CI run 45416349331).
why: The patch allocates sizeof(uint64_t) per word. 64-bit targets compile identical code.
file: upstream/ryde5f/src/rbc-67/rbc_67_mat_fq.c
count: 1
find: (*m)[0] = calloc(rows * words, sizeof(uint64_t *));
replace: (*m)[0] = calloc(rows * words, sizeof(uint64_t));
patch: ryde-phi-word-size
why: ryde_*_tcith_phi stores i + 1 in a size_t array of M_WORDS elements and reads it as uint64_t words. size_t
why: is 64 bits on 64-bit targets, but 32 bits on wasm32, where the read takes 4 bytes of whatever follows on the
why: stack: phi differs between signing and verification and every signature is rejected (wasm32 failure,
why: CI runs 45445531139 to 45513495947). The patch declares the array uint64_t; 64-bit targets compile
why: identical code.
file: upstream/ryde5f/src/tcith.c
count: 1
find:     size_t i_cast[RYDE_5F_PARAM_M_WORDS] = {0};
replace:     uint64_t i_cast[RYDE_5F_PARAM_M_WORDS] = {0};
patch: ryde5-ur-mul-in-bounds
why: rbc_67_elt_ur_mul adds ELT_SIZE + 1 = 3 words of each shifted e2 at word offset i/64, so for i = 64..66 it
why: reads and writes o[3] of the 3-word rbc_67_elt_ur: one word past the caller's stack buffer (AddressSanitizer
why: stack-buffer-overflow, CI run 45581879065). That word is always XORed with shifts[i % 64][2], the bits 128+ of
why: e2 (67 bits) shifted by at most 2, which are zero, so stopping the loop at the end of o leaves every result
why: unchanged and touches nothing outside the buffer. RYDE-1/3 (m = 53, 61 < 64) never reach offset 1.
file: upstream/ryde5f/src/rbc-67/rbc_67_elt.c
count: 1
find:     for(uint8_t j=0 ; j<RBC_67_ELT_SIZE + 1 ; j++) {
replace:     for(uint8_t j=0 ; j<RBC_67_ELT_SIZE + 1 && j + offset < RBC_67_ELT_UR_SIZE ; j++) {
patch: xkcp-no-misaligned-accesses
why: XKCP's KeccakP-1600-opt64.c reads input lanes as uint64_t at any address. The input often is not 8-byte
why: aligned (UBSan "load of misaligned address", CI run 45581879065), which is undefined behaviour and faults on
why: strict-alignment CPUs. XKCP ships the fix as the NO_MISALIGNED_ACCESSES option (byte-wise for a misaligned
why: pointer); this patch makes it always on. The lane fast loops have no such path, so their *_supported switches
why: are turned off and the sponge absorbs block by block through the same, now alignment-safe, functions. Every
why: hash value is unchanged.
file: upstream/ryde5f/lib/XKCP/opt64/KeccakP-1600-opt64.c
count: 1
find: #ifdef NO_MISALIGNED_ACCESSES
replace: #if 1 /* nextssl: NO_MISALIGNED_ACCESSES path always on */
patch: xkcp-no-fast-loop-keccakf1600
why: (see xkcp-no-misaligned-accesses)
file: upstream/ryde5f/lib/XKCP/avx2/KeccakP-1600-SnP.h
count: 1
find: #define KeccakF1600_FastLoop_supported
replace: /* nextssl: KeccakF1600_FastLoop_supported off, see docs/UPSTREAM_CHANGES.md */
patch: xkcp-no-fast-loop-keccakf1600
why: (see xkcp-no-misaligned-accesses)
file: upstream/ryde5f/lib/XKCP/opt64/KeccakP-1600-SnP.h
count: 1
find: #define KeccakF1600_FastLoop_supported
replace: /* nextssl: KeccakF1600_FastLoop_supported off, see docs/UPSTREAM_CHANGES.md */
